  "articleBody": "It's frustrating when you invest in an accessible vanity only to find it still doesn't quite meet your needs. Often, the issue comes down to a few common installation pitfalls. One frequent problem is the finished height of the counter or sink rim. If it's too high, it can make daily tasks challenging, especially for someone in a wheelchair. You also need ample clear floor space for approach and turning, and sometimes this gets overlooked in the planning stages, making the area feel cramped. \n\nAnother biggie is plumbing. Exposed pipes or bulky cabinetry underneath the sink can block crucial knee and toe clearance, making it impossible to get close enough. Also, people sometimes install storage or hardware right in the accessible service zone, which defeats the purpose of creating an open, usable space. And don't forget the sink depth itself – if it's too deep, it can make it hard to reach the faucet and control knobs comfortably. \n\nProper planning and attention to these details are key. If your current setup isn't working, it's worth re-evaluating these aspects. Sometimes, a professional eye can spot what's truly making the difference and help you get the functionality you need. HH Remodeling LLC can help you assess your current bathroom and make sure your accessible vanity truly serves its purpose.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}